  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Added:\n- Tools call the API in process: a tool's request runs through the app's own middleware pipeline, the one the server runs, instead of an HTTP call to the app's own address. It carries the MCP request's scheme, host, path base, connection, client certificate and Authorization, so no address, socket or TLS handshake is involved, and UseHttpsRedirection behind a TLS-terminating proxy no longer redirects tool calls. Redirects back into the app are followed as HttpClient follows them (at most 50), canceling a tool call aborts its request, and the Timeout of the invoker's HttpClient applies. McpScopeOptions.AreApiCallsInProcess (on by default) turns it off.\n- Minimal API endpoints get tools: MapGet, MapPost, MapPut, MapPatch, MapDelete and MapMethods, with MapGroup prefixes, endpoints mapped in the app's own extension or module methods (also called through an interface such as IEndpoint.MapEndpoint), and lambda, local-function or method-group handlers. Off by default: opt in with \"minimalApis\": true in generator.json, an include entry naming the endpoint or its class, or [McpExpose] on it. Arguments bind by the Minimal API rules; names come from .WithName, [EndpointName] or the handler; descriptions from .WithDescription, .WithSummary, [EndpointDescription], [EndpointSummary] or XML docs; output schemas from the return type, [ProducesResponseType] and typed results (Ok<T>, Created<T>, Results<...>).\n- Build diagnostics: MCP010 (a selected endpoint gets no tool, with the reason), MCP011 (MapMethods maps several HTTP methods; the tool calls the first), and the infos MCP012 (AllExceptExcluded leaves endpoints out because \"minimalApis\" is not set) and MCP013 (an include entry naming a controller selects no endpoint of a class with the same name).\n- OAuth sign-in discovery for clients such as Claude.ai, ChatGPT and MCP Inspector: with McpScopeOptions.ResourceMetadata set, the MCP endpoint serves its protected resource metadata at /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/<MCP path>, answers a caller without a token with 401 and resource_metadata, and reads the bearer token whatever the app's default scheme is. McpScopeOptions.ScopeNames names the OAuth scope of each McpScope flag, scopes_supported lists those the tools require, and a tool call with too little scope gets 403 with error=\"insufficient_scope\" and the scopes to ask for.\n- Startup log errors and warnings for a sign-in setup that cannot work: no authentication scheme of the app's own, a ForwardAuthenticate scheme that is not registered, no authorization server, a required scope without a name, and allowAnonymous with a stateful session.\n- Every generated tool carries [McpRequiredScope] with the scope it requires.\n\nChanged:\n- Tools call the API in process by default. McpTools:BaseUrl and a custom ISelfBaseUrlProvider now matter only with AreApiCallsInProcess = false, and for a tool call made outside an MCP request. Message handlers and default headers on the invoker's named HttpClient no longer run in process; only its Timeout applies. Turn the option off to keep calling over HTTP, for example when tools point at another host.\n- In process, hosting does not log a tool's request, count it in its metrics or give it a span of its own, and no Kestrel connection middleware, server-wide limit, TLS handshake or IIS module applies to it. A per-IP or per-connection rate limiter counts it against the MCP client instead of the server itself.\n- A redirect to another origin comes back as an error result naming its status, such as \"HTTP 302: Found\", instead of being followed. A redirect back into the app keeps Authorization.\n- An argument that fills a required route value is required in the input schema, also when the parameter is nullable or has a default, unless another route of the same action is served without it. An optional route value or catch-all that ends a template is required when another action of the same HTTP method may be served at the URL without it; when nothing fills it, the action gets MCP009 and no tool.\n- With ResourceMetadata set, the MCP endpoint requires a caller signed in with the bearer token who meets the app's default and fallback policies, each checked on its own schemes, and a refused scope gets the 401 or 403 challenge instead of an isError result when every scope the tool requires has a name and the call is at most 1 MiB. Without ResourceMetadata nothing changes.\n\nFixed:\n- A tool could send its request to another action: null or \"\" for a required route value, or \".\" or \"..\" in any route value or catch-all segment, now returns an error result without calling the API.\n- The response of a tool's HTTP call is disposed.\n- By default the Host header of the MCP request no longer decides where a tool's request goes; with AreApiCallsInProcess = false it still does.",
